 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "gittin.19a.6",
   "daf": "19a",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ דְּיוֹ – דְּיוֹתָא. סַם – סַמָּא. סִיקְרָא – אָמַר רַבָּה בַּר בַּר חָנָה: סְקַרְתָּא שְׁמַהּ. קוֹמוֹס – קוֹמָא. קַנְקַנְתּוֹם – אָמַר רַבָּה בַּר בַּר חָנָה אָמַר שְׁמוּאֵל: חַרְתָּא דְאוּשְׁכָּפֵי.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The Gemara begins by explaining the terms used in the mishna: «Deyo» refers to what is called «deyota,» ink, in Aramaic. «Sam» refers to «samma,» arsenic. With regard to «sikra, Rabba bar bar Ḥana said: Its name» in Aramaic is «sikreta,» red dye, derived from minium, which is also known as red lead. «Komos,» tree resin, is known as «kuma» in Aramaic. With regard to «kankantom, Rabba bar bar Ḥana said» that «Shmuel said:» This is the «black» substance «used by cobblers,» iron sulfate."
  }
 ],
